WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.870
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:10.920
Today we are going to look at a paper from
three years ago, and not any kind of paper,

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:17.620
but my kind of paper, which is in the intersection
of machine learning, computer graphics, and

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:19.440
physics simulations.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:26.380
This work zooms in on reproducing reference
motions, but, with a twist, and adds lots

00:00:26.380 --> 00:00:28.060
of amazing additional features.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.310
So what does all this mean?

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:36.660
You see, we are given this virtual character,
a reference motion that we wish to teach it,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.960
and here, additionally, we are given a task
that needs to be done.

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:46.140
So, when the reference motion is specified,
we place our AI into a physics simulation

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:49.690
where it tries to reproduce these motions.

00:00:49.690 --> 00:00:55.110
That is a good thing because if it would try
to learn to run by itself alone, it would

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:59.309
look something like this.

00:00:59.309 --> 00:01:05.530
And if we ask it to mimic the reference motion,
oh yes…much better.

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.571
Now that we have built up confidence in this
technique, let’s think bigger, and perform

00:01:10.571 --> 00:01:12.490
a backflip.

00:01:12.490 --> 00:01:13.490
Uh-oh.

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:17.590
Well, that didn’t quite work.

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:19.240
Why is that?

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:24.320
We just established that we can give it a
reference motion and it can learn it by itself.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:31.000
Well, this chap failed to learn a backflip
because it explored many motions during training,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.060
most of which resulted in failure.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:39.260
So it didn’t find a good solution and settled
for a mediocre solution instead.

00:01:39.260 --> 00:01:45.970
A proposed technique by the name Reference
State Initialization, RSI in short remedies

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:50.550
this issue by letting the agent explore better
during the training phase.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:57.980
Got it, so we add this RSI, and now, all is
well, right?

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.510
Let’s see.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:00.510
Ouch.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.670
Not so much!

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.229
It appears to fall on the ground and tries
to continue the motion from there.

00:02:06.229 --> 00:02:12.230
A+ for effort, little AI, but unfortunately
that’s not what we are looking for.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:14.140
So what is the issue here?

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:19.920
The issue is that the agent has hit the ground,
and after that, it still tries to score some

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.890
additional points by continuing to mimic the
reference motion.

00:02:23.890 --> 00:02:30.480
Again, A+ for effort, but this should not
give the agent additional scores.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:33.860
This method we just described is called early
termination.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:35.750
Let’s try it!

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:41.630
Now, we add the early termination and RSI
together, and let’s see if this will do

00:02:41.630 --> 00:02:42.890
the trick!

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.090
…And…yes!

00:02:44.090 --> 00:02:51.470
Finally, with these two additions, it can
now perform that sweet sweet backflip, rolls,

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.910
and much, much more with flying colors.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:03:00.520
So now, the agent has the basics down, and
can even perform explosive, dynamic motions

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:01.520
as well.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.860
So, it is time.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:09.340
Now hold on to your papers as now comes the
coolest part - we can perform different kinds

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:10.801
of retargeting as well.

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:13.500
What is that?

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.349
Well, one kind is retargeting the environment.

00:03:17.349 --> 00:03:23.420
This means that we can teach the AI a landing
motion in an idealized case, and then, ask

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.940
it to perform the same, but now, off of a
tall ledge.

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:33.820
Or, we can teach it to run, and then drop
it into computer game level and see if it

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:35.980
performs well.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:37.580
And it really does.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:38.580
Amazing!

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:44.330
This part is very important because in any
reasonable industry use, these characters

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:49.650
have to perform in a variety of environments
that are different from the training environment.

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:55.349
Two is retargeting not the environment, but
the body type.

00:03:55.349 --> 00:04:00.040
We can have different types of characters
learn the same motions.

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:05.599
This is pretty nice for the Atlas robot, which
has a drastically different weight distribution,

00:04:05.599 --> 00:04:09.690
and you can also see that the technique is
robust against perturbations.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:16.220
Yes, this means one of the favorite pastimes
of a computer graphics researcher, which is

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:21.900
throwing boxes at virtual characters and seeing
how well it can take it.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.850
Might as well make sure of the fact that in
a simulated world, we make up all the rules!

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.599
This one is doing really well, … oh.

00:04:31.599 --> 00:04:37.129
Note that the Atlas robot is indeed different
than the previous model, and these motions

00:04:37.129 --> 00:04:42.300
can be retargeted to it, however, this is
also a humanoid.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:45.289
Can we ask for non-humanoids as well perhaps?

00:04:45.289 --> 00:04:46.830
Oh yes!

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:54.430
This technique supports retargeting to T-Rexes,
dragons, lions, you name it.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:59.599
It can even get used to the gravity of different
virtual planets that we dream up.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:00.599
Bravo!

00:05:00.599 --> 00:05:05.380
So the value proposition of this paper is
just completely out of this world.

00:05:05.380 --> 00:05:11.680
Reference State Initialization, Early Termination,
retargeting to different body types, environments,

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:13.199
oh my!

00:05:13.199 --> 00:05:18.820
To have digital applications, like computer
games use this would already be amazing, and

00:05:18.820 --> 00:05:24.539
just imagine what we could do if we could
deploy these to real-world robots.

00:05:24.539 --> 00:05:29.460
And don’t forget, these research works just
keep on improving every year.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.249
The First Law Of Papers says that research
is a process.

00:05:33.249 --> 00:05:38.939
Do not look at where we are, look at where
we will be two more papers down the line.

00:05:38.939 --> 00:05:42.710
Now, fortunately, we can do that right now!

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:43.729
Why is that?

00:05:43.729 --> 00:05:50.069
It is because this paper is from 2018, which
means that followup papers already exist.

00:05:50.069 --> 00:05:55.309
What’s more, we even discussed one that
teaches these agents to not only reproduce

00:05:55.309 --> 00:05:59.659
these reference motions, but to do those with
style.

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:04.939
And style there meant that the agent is allowed
to make creative deviations from the reference

00:06:04.939 --> 00:06:08.979
motion, thus, developing its own way of doing
it.

00:06:08.979 --> 00:06:11.189
An amazing improvement.

00:06:11.189 --> 00:06:14.469
And I wonder what researchers will come up
with in the near future?

00:06:14.469 --> 00:06:18.229
If you have some ideas, let me know in the
comments below.

00:06:18.229 --> 00:07:04.819
What a time to be alive!

00:07:04.819 --> 00:07:13.819
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

